  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1025 AM MDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES THROUGH THE END OF THE WEEK:\n\nThe current synoptic level pattern is characterized by a high \npressure dome to our east, with troughing dominating the West Coast. \nThis will allow flow aloft over the Desert Southwest and Southern \nGreat Basin to transition to a more southerly-southwesterly regime. \nBecause of the clockwise circulation associated with high pressure \nsystems, this a prime setup for moisture advection. Recent sounding \nprofiles have been depicting consistent long, skinny CAPE profiles \nwhich are indicative of favorable precipitation rates. Today, \nsaturation down to the surface is expected, with widespread showers \nand thunderstorms in the afternoon, and wetting rains across the \nhigher elevations. This will lead to an increased threat for flash \nflooding and burn scar debris flows today and Thursday.\n\nBy Thursday, shower and storm coverage is expected to peak. Hence, \nchances for heavy rains, flash flooding, and debris flows also peaks \nthen. During these monsoonal patterns, some uncertainties can \npresent themselves when it comes to the forecast... \n\n1. Cloud Cover \n\nWith these setups, morning cloud cover frequently lingers, much like \nwe've seen over the past few days, and will serve as a primary \ndriver for afternoon storm evolution. Robust daytime heating will \nsupport more convective thunderstorm development. Conversely, \npersistent cloud cover will limit instability, favoring a shift \ntoward broader stratiform showers. Lingering cloudiness will also \ndelay convective initiation, pushing storm development deeper into \nthe afternoon. Therefore, a lot can be dependent on how cloud cover \npans out for the day.\n\n2. Lift\n\nStorms will likely initialize along the higher terrain over the next \nfew days, which is typical for our CWA. However, subtle shortwaves \nrotating around the edge of the upper level high could supply \nadditional mid-level lift. Lower confidence remains regarding the \nprecise timing and impacts of these impulses and lingering outflow \nboundaries, creating uncertainty in convective coverage and \nevolution once storms track off the ridges.\n\nWith all that being said, confidence is high that we're moving into \na wetter period for the latter half of the week and even into the \nweekend.\n\nQUICK NOTE ON TEMPERATURES:\n\nTemperatures begin to cool a few degrees each day as showers, \nstorms, and cloud cover begin to prevail. By Friday, daytime highs \nwill be below normal, a welcome relief from the previous stretch of \nhot temperatures.",
